00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.020
On July 12, 2017, Amtrak got a new CEO.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.440
The American government-owned railroad company
would now be helmed by Richard Anderson.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.430
Anderson was no stranger to the American transport
industry.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:27.490
He had previously worked for nine years as
the CEO of Delta Airlines—leading it from

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.289
bankruptcy to prosperity.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:33.270
This job, of turning around a struggling company,
is rarely a popular one.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.461
It requires making difficult decisions that
plenty will not agree with, but it’s a job

00:00:37.461 --> 00:00:39.910
that some leaders have a knack for.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:44.380
Having succeeded with this at Delta, there
was at least some evidence that Richard Anderson

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.649
could complete the same task elsewhere.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:49.670
That’s why he was brought to Amtrak.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.450
Amtrak is certainly a company in need of turnaround.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.410
While it is government owned, it is intended
to operate as a for-profit company.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:03.110
Despite that, however, it has never turned
a profit since its inception in 1971.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.350
Each year, the difference between what it
makes from running its trains and what it

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.280
costs to run those trains is made up by government
subsidies, grants, and debt.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.360
The company’s primary purpose, beyond making
money, is, of course, to carry passengers,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.130
and they don’t even do that all that well.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:22.400
In 2018, only 73% of their trains arrived
on time.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.970
In the case of their long-distance trains,
just 43% got to their destination on time.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.490
In fairness, the majority of the company’s
delays are the fault of the freight rail companies

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.700
that own the tracks that carry their trains,
but still, it’s sure that the company could

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.070
use some work.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.210
So, Richard Anderson has been getting to work.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.780
To understand how Amtrak works as a company,
you have to understand the types of trains

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.010
it operates.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.180
There are essentially three categories.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.310
The first are the Northeast Corridor trains.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.610
This route, connecting Boston, New York, DC,
and a number of smaller cities up and down

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:00.579
the east coast, is operated by both higher
speed Acela and slower Northeast Regional

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:05.740
trains, and overall, the Northeast Corridor
is by far Amtrak’s most profitable route.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:11.080
The slower, regional trains they operate earn
them a profit of almost $25 per passenger.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.560
On the higher-speed Acela, they profit more
than $80 per passenger.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.310
Without this route, Amtrak would be in a far
poorer financial state.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.239
The second type of trains are the state-supported
ones.

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:27.849
Essentially, what these are are shorter routes
supported by the subsidies offered by state

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:28.859
governments.

00:02:28.859 --> 00:02:32.959
Every single short-distance train outside
the northeast corridor is state-supported,

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.959
and this type includes routes like Charlotte
to Raleigh, North Carolina; Chicago to Quincy,

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:39.689
Illinois; or Vancouver, Canada to Portland,
Oregon.

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:41.659
These routes range in profitability.

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:47.730
The DC to Lynchburg train, for example, earns
the company almost $40 in profit per passenger.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:53.390
Meanwhile, the Fort Worth, Texas, to Oklahoma
City, Oklahoma one loses more than $30 per

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:54.390
passenger.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:59.419
Overall, though, the state-supported routes
as a grouping are unprofitable, but not by

00:02:59.419 --> 00:03:00.419
that much.

00:03:00.419 --> 00:03:03.139
They are somewhat close to break-even.

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:06.790
The third type of trains is the long-distance
ones.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:12.260
Ranging from 13 hours to 65, these routes
connect big cities to small towns all across

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:13.560
the US.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.919
Many of these routes have storied histories
and strong fanbases of those who prefer the

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:23.359
more relaxed method of travel, however, these
routes certainly do not help Amtrak’s financial

00:03:23.359 --> 00:03:24.849
performance.

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:28.699
Every single one of Amtrak’s long-distance
routes lose money.

00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:33.400
How much money they lose ranges anywhere from
$12 per passenger in the case of the Palmetto

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.489
between New York City and Savannah, Georgia
all the way to $456 per passenger in the case

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:43.230
of the Sunset Limited from New Orleans, Louisiana
to Los Angeles.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:49.310
Overall, Amtrak loses more than a half billion
dollars a year operating these long-distance

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.639
routes.

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:56.029
Part of what makes Richard Anderson the perfect
figure to turn around Amtrak is that he does

00:03:56.029 --> 00:03:57.869
not care about trains.

00:03:57.869 --> 00:04:03.869
That’s to say, he has no sense of sentimentality
about the history or the grandeur or the allure

00:04:03.869 --> 00:04:06.430
of particular storied routes.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:11.980
His passion is for profitability and that’s
his goal, seemingly no matter the cost.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.651
Therefore, three core parts to Anderson’s
Amtrak plan have emerged—expanding the Northeast

00:04:17.651 --> 00:04:23.040
Corridor services, optimizing the state-supported
services, and cutting back the long-distance

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.669
services.

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:30.080
Starting with the Northeast Corridor, this
is clearly Amtrak’s greatest asset.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.031
The company owns most of the tracks between
DC and Boston meaning that they don’t run

00:04:34.031 --> 00:04:39.340
into the same problems of using freight railroad’s
tracks like with almost all their other services.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.669
This means they can run these services relatively
reliably, and therefore have a clear advantage

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:47.310
over planes or buses when traveling between
New York and DC or Boston.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:52.319
Consequently, they have cornered the market
between Boston, New York, and DC.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:56.590
The first part of Amtrak’s plan to double-down
on this route was initiated before Anderson

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.110
even joined the company.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:03.250
Amtrak is receiving new, faster, and larger
train sets for their higher-speed Acela Express

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.900
service on the route in 2021.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.620
With the introduction of these, Amtrak has
teased plans to create four tiers of transport

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.199
for the corridor.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:17.360
The lowest would be a new, local train operating
all the way from Richmond, Virginia to Portland,

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.680
Maine making most all stops along the way.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.919
This idea for this sort of, “super-local,”
train would be to connect additional smaller

00:05:23.919 --> 00:05:28.349
communities to the Amtrak Network, as this
service could be used to connect to other

00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:29.699
higher-speed trains.

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:33.610
The next tier up would be the existing Northeast
Regional service, operating between Boston

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.689
and DC, with some continuing on into Virginia.

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:41.200
Above that would be the current Acela Express,
operating limited-stop service between Boston

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.040
and DC, then above that would be new, nonstop
services from DC and Boston to New York.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.449
The company launched the first of these in
September, 2019, with a travel time of 2 hours

00:05:51.449 --> 00:05:56.289
and 35 minutes—shaving about 15 minutes
off the travel time of the stopping Acelas.

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:01.659
In the future, the addition of nonstop services
from Boston and additional frequencies from

00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:06.469
DC will cement this as the most premium option
in the Northeast Corridor, and the idea is

00:06:06.469 --> 00:06:12.509
that these four tiered options together will
help capture more of every customer segment—thereby

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:16.219
squeezing more revenue out of Amtrak’s most
profitable route.

00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:21.639
The fact that some of Amtrak’s state-supported
trains break even is a fantastic sign because

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:27.430
it means, with optimization, that Amtrak can
turn this grouping profitable.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:32.610
Anderson has made noise about expanding Amtrak’s
short-distance network across the US.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:37.200
The company has not announced any specific
new routes as part of a major short-distance

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.169
expansion, but it’s clear that there are
opportunities.

00:06:41.169 --> 00:06:45.759
Trains tend to be competitive in time and
cost to flights under a distance of about

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:48.569
300 miles or 500 kilometers.

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:53.289
That means there are plenty of likely profitable
routes that Amtrak could set up, like Dallas

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:55.599
to Houston or Los Angeles to Las Vegas.

00:06:55.599 --> 00:07:00.340
While it’s unlikely that Amtrak would set
up any additional short-distance routes without

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.060
state funding, the company does have potential
future competition.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:09.660
Two private companies are fairly serious about
starting up high-speed train service on these

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:10.819
routes.

00:07:10.819 --> 00:07:15.069
Between Dallas and Houston, a company called
Texas Central Railway is months away from

00:07:15.069 --> 00:07:19.930
starting construction on a high-speed line
connecting the cities in just 90 minutes.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.539
Elsewhere, Virgin Trains USA—the company
that owns what is currently the only privately-owned

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:29.349
inter-city railroad in the US between Miami,
Fort Lauderdale, and West Palm Beach—recently

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:33.249
acquired a company that had begun planning
and permitting work for a Los Angeles area

00:07:33.249 --> 00:07:38.029
to Las Vegas high-speed line, and is looking
to start construction in 2020.

00:07:38.029 --> 00:07:43.000
If these projects are completed, which seems
quite possible, two of Amtrak’s most potentially

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.889
profitable routes would be snapped up and,
if successful, it would open the door to future

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:50.180
private rail projects in the US.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:54.760
While significant rail investment and development
would no doubt prove quite beneficial for

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:59.690
the country and help solve many of its transport
problems, to Amtrak, it would get in the way

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:04.680
of their ability to cherry-pick the most profitable
short-haul routes in order to offset the loss

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.560
of long-distance routes.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.030
That brings us to those long-distance routes.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:13.099
This is where things get tricky for Amtrak.

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:17.719
As a publicly-owned company enjoying quite
sizable amounts of public funding, Amtrak

00:08:17.719 --> 00:08:21.610
relies on keeping in the good graces of the
US Congress.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:26.309
Their long-distance routes pass through more
than 40 states and cutting them would be politically

00:08:26.309 --> 00:08:28.360
unpopular anywhere.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.140
Not only do they serve as economic stimulus
by providing jobs to rural areas, but many

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:37.699
of the company’s 500 stops represent the
only public transport link to the outside

00:08:37.699 --> 00:08:40.269
world from the small towns they’re in.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:44.829
Congresspeople are well aware that the loss
of a route that their constituents use would

00:08:44.829 --> 00:08:46.550
not help with their popularity.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:51.490
At the very same time, Congress has been putting
the pressure on Amtrak to cut its losses,

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:56.209
even though the very thing loosing all their
money are these long-distance routes.

00:08:56.209 --> 00:09:01.600
Therefore, for now, the company has started
making efforts to cut cost on these trains.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.380
As one example, they’ve started to move
their long-distance trains away from serving

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:10.320
fresh meals from their onboard kitchen towards
stocking pre-packaged, pre-prepared foods.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.730
This, undoubtably, did not go down well with
Amtrak loyalists, but the whole long-distance

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.519
mess gets even messier.

00:09:18.519 --> 00:09:22.990
The company has been accused of, essentially,
misleading accounting practices.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:28.139
This would presumably be in order to make
the long-distance routes seem like bigger

00:09:28.139 --> 00:09:33.390
money losers than they truly are in order
to help the case for their discontinuation.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:37.900
The company attributes different costs to
different lines to give a sense of which make

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.190
money and which don’t, but this isn’t
always done well.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:46.550
For example, it apparently cost $3 million
a year to maintain the company’s electric

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:51.180
train equipment outside the northeast corridor
even though, aside from a small branch line

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.370
to Harrisburg, there are no Amtrak electric
train routes outside the northeast corridor—only

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:57.440
diesel.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.560
It also attributed all the cost of its baggage
handling services to its long-distance trains,

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.370
even though these are used on many short-distance
services as well.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:10.750
The biggest highlight of their cost attribution,
though, is what they marked down as the cost

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:15.760
they bore for their station in Miami—the
terminus of some of the long-distance routes—to

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.709
be cleaned of snow.

00:10:17.709 --> 00:10:21.550
The last time it snowed anywhere close to
Miami was 1977.

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:26.690
What’s likely to happen, in the near future,
is that Amtrak will cut the most egregious

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:31.400
losers in its long-distance portfolio while
keeping those that are closer to break-even

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.410
in order to appease Congress.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.520
The termination of any route would be a huge
loss to many.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.310
They each provide hundreds or thousands of
jobs and connect many of the country’s smallest

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:47.570
towns to bigger cities, but its likely a necessity
given the pressure from DC.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:52.070
The new leader of Amtrak, Richard Anderson,
has, though, helped lead the company towards

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.130
the goal that long seemed impossible.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:11:00.310
In the company’s last fiscal year, it lost
just $30 million.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:06.620
In Amtrak terms, that is nothing, and it puts
them on track to break-even in 2020—an achievement

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:11.260
that the company has never reached since its
inception 50 years ago.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:15.750
This will occur during what is clearly a time
of great change for Amtrak.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.920
It is reinventing itself at the beginning
of a period of reinvention for the American

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.630
rail industry as a whole.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:26.690
What many are worried about, though, is that
it and the Congresspeople behind it will forget

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:30.650
the difference between a private company and
a government-owned one.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:36.699
If Amtrak was a true for-profit company, it
would have long ago made the quite easy decision

00:11:36.699 --> 00:11:40.040
to cut every single long-distance route.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:45.170
Doing so would immediately turn them into
a quite profitable corporation, but the company’s

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:50.569
purpose is to connect America, and there’s
a whole lot more to America than a four-hour

00:11:50.569 --> 00:11:53.970
radius around the largest cities.

